          "body": "## Scope\n\nCloses #366. Independent follow-up to #363. No production fixes, unmerged expression work, or literal-default decisions.\n\nStart with `research/concurrency/README.md` and `SPEC.md`. The seven configurations retain 61 controlled concurrent scenarios, seven no-op controls, actual connection settings, SQL, and installed DDL.\n\n## Findings\n\n- Separate read/write transactions permitted stale assignment to leave 9 after two requested decrements of 10. Unguarded callers treated clean completion as success. Native snekql/MariaDB returned 0 for the second unchanged write, an available hint but not automatic optimistic checking.\n- Atomic arithmetic left 8 everywhere. The tested snekql builder required its public raw path for arithmetic; SQLAlchemy used update expressions.\n- Explicit revision predicates detected stale writes. Matched CAS increments revision, so 1/0 counts agreed despite MariaDB no-op rowcount differences.\n- SQLite SQLAlchemy legacy mode reported an active Session transaction while its driver had no transaction after SELECT. Explicit BEGIN exposed stale writes as SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT instead of losing the decrement.\n- The initial MariaDB server reported snapshot isolation enabled. With it on, both libraries exposed error 1020 for obsolete in-transaction reads. With it off, REPEATABLE READ allowed the unguarded overwrite. Both configurations are retained rather than attributing a server policy to a library.\n- SQLAlchemy version_id_col detected stale object flushes through StaleDataError. Explicit Session.execute(update(...)) bypassed that mapper policy and still lost an update.\n- Known held locks produced SQLite code 5 or MariaDB code 1205. One explicit full-transaction retry reread quantity 9/revision 2 and safely reached quantity 8/revision 3. Retrying stale parameters was not the policy.\n\n## Method\n\nBarriers/events establish both old reads, commit ordering, and holder release. No correctness sleeps. Real independent transactions/sessions, fresh readback, owned temporary databases, and outer schedule deadlines. Unexpected errors or ambiguous commit failures abort instead of being counted as acceptable conflicts.\n\nThis is controlled interleaving, not a stress test or race-frequency estimate. SQLite busy waits and MariaDB lock waits are deliberately bounded; BEGIN IMMEDIATE retry, deadlocks, network failures, pessimistic locking APIs, and performance remain outside scope.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Full package plus research suite: 1646 passed, including 87 research checks.\n- Clean temporary environment: 87 research checks and typing passed after installing the pinned comparator requirements.\n- Ruff lint, formatting, lock check, and diff checks passed.\n- CI installs the comparator separately and uses explicit test paths. No root dependency/lockfile changes.\n- Typing exception limited to SQLAlchemy mapped_column declarations in one research file.\n\nThe report separates native behavior, added connection policy, mapper policy, and application retry decisions. It makes no overall library ranking or general serializability claim.\n",
